summary\": \"Tumor grade is explicitly derived from histology and expressed through standardized categories (GX–G3)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"GRADE is defined as a cancer characteristic that reflects how abnormal tumor cells appear under the microscope, and it is explicitly stated to be based on HISTOLOGY. This makes grade a structured interpretive output of microscopic assessment rather than a separate or independent measurement. [Data: Entities (3893, 1055); Relationships (8143)].\\n\\nThe grading framework is expanded through G CATEGORY (G SCORE) and specific grade codes: GX (grade cannot be determined), G1 (low grade), G2 (intermediate), and G3 (high grade). These categories operationalize aggressiveness characterization in a standardized way and can be used across reporting and clinical discussions. [Data: Entities (5351, 3918, 3919, 3920, 3921); Relationships (8146, 8147, 8148, 8149, 11852)].\\n\\nBecause these grade terms are tied to microscope-based abnormality assessment, the grading system is part of the community’s technical lexicon and may contribute to patient confusion if not explained alongside the underlying histologic rationale. [Data: Entities (3893, 5351); Relationships (11852, 8143)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Abbreviations in pathology reports are a notable complexity that requires clinician interpretation\",\n      \"explanation\": \"PATHOLOGY REPORTS are noted to commonly include many abbreviations and technical notations, and ABBREVIATIONS are described as dense shorthand used in staging/pathology reporting (including examples such as TNM variants and more nuanced notations). This establishes that report readability is a recurring issue within the community. [Data: Entities (3898, 3922); Relationships (8162)].\\n\\nSpecific examples are explicitly connected to PATHOLOGY REPORT: pN0(mol+), ypT2, and “cNA…” are each described as example abbreviations/shorthand that may appear in reports and that care teams should explain. These 